//? Title: Contractive Autoencoders: What a Small Encoder Jacobian Means
//? Subtitle: Exact sigmoid sensitivity, complete penalty gradients, and matched 784–32–784 MNIST fits with reconstruction and collapse controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An encoder can reconstruct training observations yet change its representation sharply when its input moves.
A contractive autoencoder penalizes that local sensitivity while learning to reconstruct the original input.
This report derives the exact sigmoid-encoder Jacobian penalty and both of its parameter-gradient paths,
checks them on a two-observation example, and contrasts infinitesimal sensitivity with finite changes.
Three matched 784–32–784 networks then fit genuine MNIST with penalty coefficients 0, 0.01 and 0.1.
Validation selects each network using its own objective; held-out reconstruction error, activation saturation,
code variability and independently differentiated Jacobians qualify the resulting small derivatives.
The migrated example uses independent encoder/decoder weights and mean squared reconstruction error.
In these short runs, the strongest penalty yields the smallest encoder norm but worse reconstruction
than the constant fitting-mean image; none of the measured quantities establishes semantic invariance.
)
\toc

> The method

For an input row \(x\in\mathbb R^d\), let
\(h=\sigma(xW+b)\in\mathbb R^k\) and \(\hat x=\sigma(hV+c)\), with independent
\(W\in\mathbb R^{d\times k}\) and \(V\in\mathbb R^{k\times d}\). Both activations are logistic sigmoids.
The objective averages the reconstruction error over examples and pixels, but sums the squared
encoder derivatives over all input and code coordinates before averaging over examples:

$$
L=\frac{1}{Nd}\sum_{n,i}(\hat x_{ni}-x_{ni})^2+\lambda P,\qquad
P=\frac1N\sum_n\|J_h(x_n)\|_F^2,\qquad\lambda\ge0.
$$
\alttext(The loss is mean squared reconstruction error plus lambda times the mean squared Frobenius norm of the encoder Jacobian.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/contractive_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(55, 1150);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Observed pixels x")
      .node("h", {5, 0}, "Sigmoid code h")
      .node("r", {10, 0}, "Sigmoid reconstruction")
      .node("p", {2.5, -3}, "Encoder sensitivity\nMean squared Jacobian norm")
      .node("m", {10, -3}, "Reconstruction MSE\nTarget is x")
      .node("l", {6, -6}, "Loss = MSE + λ P")
      .edge("x", "h", "Encoder W, b").edge("h", "r", "Decoder V, c")
      .edge("h", "p", "Slopes and W").edge("r", "m")
      .edge("p", "l", "lambda P").edge("m", "l", "MSE")
      .text({5, 2}, "The penalty differentiates through both sigmoid slopes and encoder weights")
      .save_svg("build/contractive_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/contractive_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The penalty measures the encoder at the observed input. Reconstruction and regularization share the encoder; fitting remains an explicit optimizer loop.)
\alttext(Input pixels pass through a sigmoid encoder and sigmoid decoder. Encoder slopes and weights feed a sensitivity penalty, which joins reconstruction MSE in the loss.)

Write \(s_{nj}=h_{nj}(1-h_{nj})\) and \(q_j=\sum_iW_{ij}^2\). Since
\(\partial h_j/\partial x_i=s_jW_{ij}\), the exact penalty is

$$
P=\frac1N\sum_{n,j}s_{nj}^2q_j.
$$
\alttext(The penalty is the batch average of squared sigmoid slopes multiplied by squared encoder weight column norms.)

Computing the column norms costs \(O(dk)\), and combining them with a batch of activations costs
\(O(Nk)\). There is no need to allocate a full \(N\times k\times d\) Jacobian to train the model.
The forward/reverse network cost remains \(O(Ndk)\), with parameters and activations requiring
\(O(dk+N(d+k))\) storage. Full Jacobians are used below only as independent small-probe checks.

The gradient must include the direct dependence of \(q_j\) on \(W_{ij}\), as well as the dependence
through \(h\). Applying the chain rule gives

$$
\frac{\partial P}{\partial W_{ij}}=\frac{2}{N}\sum_n
\left[s_{nj}^2W_{ij}+s_{nj}^2(1-2h_{nj})q_jx_{ni}\right],\qquad
\frac{\partial P}{\partial b_j}=\frac{2}{N}\sum_n s_{nj}^2(1-2h_{nj})q_j.
$$
\alttext(The encoder weight derivative has a direct term from its column norm and an indirect term through the sigmoid slope. Bias derivatives contain only the latter path.)

Detaching the weight norms discards the first term. Detaching the activations discards the second.
The canonical `dm::nn::models::SigmoidEncoderContraction` preserves both paths and supports higher
parameter derivatives. `ContractiveAutoencoder` composes the two sigmoid layers with this penalty;
it does no optimization. It accepts overcomplete code widths too, although this study retains the
source example's smaller 32-dimensional code.

This is the source example's untied-weight, pixel-MSE variant of the penalty introduced by
[Rifai et al. (2011)](https://icml.cc/Conferences/2011/papers/455_icmlpaper.pdf). Their reported
experiments use tied weights and cross-entropy reconstruction. Neither their classification results
nor their hyperparameter choices are being reproduced here. With independent weights, an encoder
and decoder can compensate for changes in representation scale; a small encoder derivative alone
therefore does not establish a useful representation.

> Two observations, with all derivatives checked

Use \(X=((0.1,0.2,0.7),(0.8,0.3,0.4))\),
\(W=((0.3,-0.4),(0.7,0.2),(-0.2,0.5))\), and \(b=(0.1,-0.2)\).
The weight-column squared norms are 0.62 and 0.45. For the first observation the logits are
\((0.13,0.15)\); for the second they are \((0.47,-0.26)\). These four sigmoids determine all
12 Jacobian entries and the two per-observation penalties.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/contractive_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto x = n::tensor({.1, .2, .7, .8, .3, .4}, {2, 3}, type).requires_grad_();
  const auto w = n::tensor({.3, -.4, .7, .2, -.2, .5}, {3, 2}, type).requires_grad_();
  const auto b = n::tensor({.1, -.2}, {2}, type).requires_grad_();
  const auto h = n::sigmoid(n::matmul(x, w) + b);
  const auto penalty = n::models::SigmoidEncoderContraction(h, w);
  n::GradOptions keep;
  keep.retain_graph = true;
  const auto gradient = n::grad(penalty.mean, {w, b}, keep);
  std::vector<n::Tensor> rows;
  for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
    rows.push_back(n::grad(n::sum(n::slice_axis(h, 1, j, j + 1)), {x}, keep)[0]);
  const auto jacobian = n::stack(rows, 1);
  const auto independent = n::sum(n::square(jacobian), {1, 2});
  const auto slope2 = n::square(h * (1. - h));
  const auto delta = slope2 * (1. - 2. * h) * n::sum(n::square(w), {0});
  const auto dw = n::matmul(n::transpose(x), delta) + w * n::sum(slope2, {0});
  const auto db = n::sum(delta, {0}); // 2/N = 1 for this batch.
  const double error = n::max(n::abs(independent - penalty.per_example)).item();
  if (error > 1e-13 || n::max(n::abs(dw - gradient[0])).item() > 1e-13 ||
      n::max(n::abs(db - gradient[1])).item() > 1e-13) return 1;
  std::print("| observation | code | all encoder derivatives | exact squared norm | autodiff squared norm |\n|---:|---|---|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.10f} | {:.10f} |\n", i,
        n::select(h, 0, i).values_str(), n::select(jacobian, 0, i).values_str(),
        penalty.per_example.at({i}), independent.at({i}));
  std::print("\nMean penalty: {:.10f}. All six weight derivatives: {}. Both bias derivatives: {}.\n",
             penalty.mean.item(), gradient[0].values_str(), gradient[1].values_str());
  const n::TensorFunction objective = [](const auto& in) {
    return n::models::SigmoidEncoderContraction(n::sigmoid(n::matmul(in[0], in[1]) + in[2]), in[1]).mean;
  };
  const auto first = n::gradcheck(objective, {x, w, b}, {.atol = 1e-8, .rtol = 1e-6});
  const auto second = n::gradgradcheck(objective, {x, w, b}, {.atol = 1e-8, .rtol = 1e-6});
  if (!first.ok || !second.ok) return 1;
  std::print("\nFirst derivative finite-difference error {:.2e}; second derivative error {:.2e}.\n", first.max_abs_error, second.max_abs_error);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(55, 1150);
  for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
    const auto suffix = std::to_string(i);
    const double y = -3. * i;
    d.node("x" + suffix, {0, y}, "x = " + n::select(x, 0, i).values_str())
        .node("h" + suffix, {6, y}, "h = " + n::select(h, 0, i).values_str())
        .node("p" + suffix, {12, y}, std::format("Squared norm = {:.6f}", independent.at({i})))
        .edge("x" + suffix, "h" + suffix).edge("h" + suffix, "p" + suffix);
  }
  d.save_svg("build/contractive_toy.svg");
}
```
