\caption(The two rows have distinct activations and local sensitivities. Each displayed squared norm sums six squared derivatives, with no division by the number of coordinates.)
\alttext(Two input triples pass into their two-coordinate sigmoid codes, then into their exact squared encoder Jacobian norms.)

> Checking what contraction does and does not promise

For a sigmoid, \(0\le s\le1/4\), hence
\(0\le\|J_h(x)\|_F^2\le\|W\|_F^2/16\). Small weights reduce this bound, but saturation
also makes the actual derivative small even when the weights are unchanged. An encoder with all
weights zero has zero penalty and a constant code; its best decoder output under MSE is only a
constant image. Sensitivity must be interpreted alongside reconstruction and code variability.

For a perturbation \(\delta\), \(h(x+\delta)-h(x)=J_h(x)\delta+O(\|\delta\|^2)\).
The penalty controls this first-order term, not arbitrary finite displacements. In one dimension,
\(h(x)=\sigma(x)\) has a small slope at \(x=5\), yet moving eight units towards the negative side
crosses its transition region. This fixed-function example has no fitted parameters.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/contractive_sensitivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto z = n::linspace(-8, 8, 161, type).reshape({161, 1});
  const auto code = n::sigmoid(z);
  const auto p = n::models::SigmoidEncoderContraction(code, n::ones({1, 1}, type));
  if (n::max(p.per_example).item() > 1. / 16 + 1e-14) return 1;
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.lines(z.to_vector<double>(), p.per_example.to_vector<double>(), "Squared sigmoid slope", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines({-8, 8}, {1. / 16, 1. / 16}, "Upper bound 1/16", {220, 38, 38}, 1.)
      .x_label("preactivation").y_label("squared input derivative").title("Saturation reduces the derivative at fixed weight");
  const double local = n::sigmoid(n::scalar(5., type)).item() * (1. - n::sigmoid(n::scalar(5., type)).item());
  std::vector<double> logs, ratios;
  std::print("| displacement towards negative x | finite contraction ratio | local slope at x=5 |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (double epsilon : {.001, .01, .1, 1., 2., 4., 8.}) {
    const double ratio = (n::sigmoid(n::scalar(5., type)) - n::sigmoid(n::scalar(5. - epsilon, type))).item() / epsilon;
    logs.push_back(std::log10(epsilon));
    ratios.push_back(ratio);
    std::print("| {:.3f} | {:.7f} | {:.7f} |\n", epsilon, ratio, local);
  }
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  right.lines(logs, ratios, "Finite displacement", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines({logs.front(), logs.back()}, {local, local}, "Infinitesimal slope", {220, 38, 38}, 1.)
      .x_label("log10 displacement magnitude").y_label("absolute code change / displacement")
      .title("A local derivative is not the finite contraction ratio");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto figure = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  figure.add(left).add(right).size(1150, 500).save_svg("build/contractive_sensitivity.svg");
}
```
