\image(figures/contractive_learning.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Reconstruction MSE and encoder sensitivity share the same matched runs, but each fit minimizes a differently weighted objective. The plots use separate vertical scales.)
\alttext(Three validation reconstruction-error curves and three encoder-Jacobian-norm curves over three epochs.)

The full probe computes 602,112 derivative entries across the three selected networks. It checks the
analytic norm against reverse-mode derivatives of the actual encoder, with one backward pass per code
coordinate. The retained first-minibatch gradients and post-update parameters additionally support an
independent scalar-chain-rule and Adam-step check. The test pool is evaluated after checkpoint selection.

All three variants select epoch 3. The strong penalty reduces the held-out squared encoder norm
from the plain model's 0.01315 to 0.00727, but its MSE is 0.06975, worse than both the plain model
(0.06851) and the fitting-mean image (0.06780). The moderate penalty gives MSE 0.06736 and a larger
code-coordinate standard deviation, 0.02860 versus 0.00985 for the plain network, yet its encoder
norm is also larger, 0.03403. Its observed MSE advantage over the constant baseline is small and
comes from one seed and split. These finite-step fits do not establish monotonic control of the
held-out Jacobian by lambda. Saturated fractions of 0.736–0.867 and the gallery below show why
small derivatives alone would be an incomplete description of these networks.

> Reconstructions and code dependence

The gallery keeps the first held-out occurrence of each digit, ordered 0 through 9. Original inputs,
plain reconstructions, lambda 0.01 reconstructions, lambda 0.1 reconstructions and the fitting-only
pixel mean use identical grayscale limits and integer enlargement. No image is chosen by reconstruction quality.
The label is used only for this presentation order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/contractive_reconstructions.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> a;
  for (const auto& [name, value] : n::load_npz("build/contractive_results.npz")) a.emplace(name, value);
  const auto labels = a.at("test_labels").to_vector<int>();
  std::vector<std::int64_t> rows;
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit) {
    const auto found = std::find(labels.begin(), labels.end(), digit);
    if (found == labels.end()) return 1;
    rows.push_back(found - labels.begin());
  }
  std::vector<n::Tensor> panels;
  for (const auto name : {"test_input", "plain_reconstruction", "contractive_reconstruction", "strong_reconstruction"})
    panels.push_back(n::take_rows(a.at(name), rows));
  panels.push_back(n::broadcast_to(a.at("pixel_mean"), {10, 784}));
  n::save_image_grid(n::concat(panels, 0).reshape({50, 28, 28, 1}), "build/contractive_reconstructions.png", 10, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3);
  std::print("Rows: originals, plain, lambda 0.01, lambda 0.1, fitting pixel mean. Columns: digits zero through nine.\n");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Rows: originals, plain, lambda 0.01, lambda 0.1, fitting pixel mean. Columns: digits zero through nine.
\image(figures/contractive_reconstructions.png)
// result_end
\caption(Aligned reconstructions and a constant-image baseline. A low sensitivity is useful only in the context of the reconstruction and variability it preserves.)
\alttext(A five-row, ten-column contact sheet of original digits, three matched reconstructions and a repeated fitting-mean image.)

> Conclusion

The exact sigmoid penalty can be trained without constructing a full Jacobian, and both its direct
weight derivative and its activation-slope derivative matter. Saturation can reduce sensitivity without
learning useful distinctions; finite changes can also cross a transition region despite a small local
slope. The matched short MNIST fits therefore report MSE, code variability and saturation alongside
encoder derivatives. No classifier, semantic invariance, finite-perturbation robustness guarantee,
disentanglement or bit-rate compression result follows from these measurements.

> References

- Rifai, S., Vincent, P., Muller, X., Glorot, X. and Bengio, Y. (2011). [Contractive Auto-Encoders:
  Explicit Invariance During Feature Extraction](https://icml.cc/Conferences/2011/papers/455_icmlpaper.pdf).
  Proceedings of ICML, pp. 833–840. The exact encoder-Jacobian penalty is used here with the migrated source's MSE and independent weights.
- LeCun, Y., Bottou, L., Bengio, Y. and Haffner, P. (1998). [Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791).
  Proceedings of the IEEE 86(11), pp. 2278–2324.
